{
  "raw": {
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a78859f98ed642e01991a3e54666c8b&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "Learning Specialist",
      "description": "Position: Learning Specialist Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 111 # of Openings: 10 At Beacon College, you'll do more than support students—you'll help transform lives. Join a collaborative team at the forefront of making higher education accessible to neurodivergent learners, developing innovative strategies that foster meaningful engagement, academic success, and independence. Guided by a holistic, student-centered philosophy, you'll have the opportunity to help shape the future of neurodivergent education while making a lasting difference in students' lives. TITLE: Learning Specialist DEPARTMENT: Center for Student Success WORK TYPE: Full-Time | Staff | Academic Year: August through May a 10-month position LOCATION: Beacon College, Leesburg, Florida FLSA: Exempt Salary (10-month pay is spread over 12-months or 26 pay periods each year) DIVISION: Academic Affairs Job Summary The Learning Specialist promotes student success by providing academic, personal, and developmental support throughout a student's college experience. Through mentoring, advising, coaching, and advocacy, this role helps students strengthen learning strategies, executive functioning, study skills, and the confidence needed to succeed in college and beyond. The Learning Specialist serves as a consistent source of support, meeting regularly with students in individual and group settings to foster academic achievement, resilience, personal growth, and independence. This role also maintains accurate documentation and collaborates with faculty, staff, administrators, families, and other stakeholders to ensure student success. Above all, the Learning Specialist demonstrates care, accountability, collaboration, and a student-centered approach that empowers students to thrive academically, socially, and personally. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Educational and Developmental Mentor Monitor students' academic performance and assist them in developing effective learning strategies, positive habits, and compensatory skills related to attendance, class participation, assignment completion, use of campus resources, and communication. Model, scaffold, and monitor the initiation and completion of academic tasks during one-on-one meetings and Open Learning sessions. Reinforce skill development in executive functioning, study strategies, technology literacy, critical thinking, and problem-solving. Identify students who may be at risk of academic, social, or campus-related challenges and provide proactive support. Develop and implement interventions to improve student success throughout the semester, including after midterm grade reviews, ensuring access to appropriate services and resources. Collaborate with departments across campus through the Triadic Model to support students' academic, social, and personal development through coordinated efforts. Utilize shared resources and information to improve efficiency and avoid duplication of services. Individualize support based on each student's academic level, developmental needs, and personal goals. Academic Advisor Communicate advising policies, procedures, and expectations clearly to students. Connect students with academic resources, including the Student Portal, Catalog, and other support services. Assist students in selecting academic programs and courses aligned with their interests, strengths, and career goals, including developmental coursework when appropriate. Help students develop balanced and realistic academic plans. Collaborate with the Director of Advising to ensure adherence to degree and program requirements. Maintain effective communication with faculty, staff, students, and families, ensuring timely responses and collaboration. Life Advisor Support students in addressing personal concerns, life challenges, and interpersonal conflicts. Help students build productive relationships and navigate the college environment effectively. Develop students' communication skills, including active listening and self-advocacy. Monitor student well-being and connect students with appropriate mental, physical, and campus wellness resources. Teach and reinforce strategies for managing stress, anxiety, and other challenges that may affect learning and success. Supporting the Transition to Independence Utilize scaffolding and gradual release strategies to build student autonomy. Guide students in planning for their academic, professional, and personal futures. Assist students in establishing and pursuing meaningful academic and life goals. Foster self-awareness, social-emotional growth, and personal responsibility. Provide guidance related to life skills and make referrals when additional support is needed. Encourage informed decision-making, accountability, and self-advocacy. Build students' confidence, competence, and independence. Recordkeeping and Documentation Following each student session, document student progress, trends, interventions, and other relevant information in Maxient. Review faculty updates and student performance information in Canvas on a weekly basis. Update the Student of Concern document weekly and provide bi-weekly updates during meetings with the Dean of Student Success and Disability Services Coordinator. Track student contacts and submit monthly activity reports to the Dean of Student Success and Disability Services Coordinator. Maintain written communication with students between meetings as needed. Update and maintain student face sheets annually, creating new documents when necessary. Prepare scheduled and as-needed communications to parents and families, with a minimum of one to three communications per semester. Other Responsibilities Serve as a student advocate and assist students with navigating accommodation processes as appropriate. Conduct weekly one-on-one meetings and facilitate Open Learning sessions. Listen actively to student concerns and provide appropriate support and guidance. Demonstrate genuine care for student well-being, belonging, and success. Encourage student engagement through campus activities, clubs, events, and community involvement. Stay current with emerging best practices in student support, coaching, and learning services, sharing knowledge and strategies with colleagues. Perform other duties as assigned. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Education: Master's degree in education, counseling, learning disabilities, or a closely related field relevant to the work of the Center for Student Success required. Experience: Minimum of three years of experience in academic support, advising, coaching, counseling, teaching, disability services, or a related educational field. Demonstrated content knowledge and pedagogical expertise required. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Advanced graduate study in education, counseling, learning disabilities, or a related field preferred. Experience working with students with learning differences in a secondary or postsecondary educational setting preferred. Experience providing academic support, coaching, advising, or instruction to students in a postsecondary educational setting preferred. Experience using Maxient or similar student success and case management software preferred. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Comprehensive understanding of the mission of Beacon College and its undergraduate support model, as well as demonstrated knowledge of contemporary research and developments in the field of learning disabilities. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite and other educational technology platforms. Effective time management skills. Strong organizational skills with the ability to prioritize tasks and manage multiple responsibilities. Excellent communication, interpersonal, decision-making, and conflict-resolution skills. Demonstrates professionalism, patience, and discretion when working with students, faculty, staff, families, and other members of the college community. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with diverse internal and external stakeholders. Flexibility to work occasional evenings and weekends for events and special projects is required. Strong service orientation with a demonstrated ability to effectively meet the needs of and work collaboratively with a diverse population of students, staff, faculty, and public. ENVIRONMENT: This role operates in a professional office environment with regular interaction across campus and digital platforms. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Additional Information This description is intended to outline the general scope of responsibilities and is not an exhaustive list of duties. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a7885a89f8b940c019fd242824e77f5&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Position: Administrative Assistant Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 202 # of Openings: 1 Make a difference in the lives of students at Beacon College. As the Administrative Assistant for the Center for College Readiness, you will support program operations, assist prospective students and families, and help coordinate outreach, recruitment, and special events in a collaborative, mission-driven environment. TITLE: Administrative Assistant DEPARTMENT: Center for College Readiness WORK TYPE: Full-Time | Staff LOCATION: Beacon College, Leesburg, Florida FLSA: Non-Exempt Hourly DIVISION: Enrollment Management JOB SUMMARY: The Administrative Assistant to the Center for College Readiness provides essential administrative support to the program directors while assisting with day-to-day management and triage of incoming emails, phone calls and associated requests. This role assists with processing incoming student documentation for college readiness program applicants, coordinating communication related to program services, and facilitates coordination with admissions and marketing teams for events, recruitment and promotion of the Center for College Readiness. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND responsibilities: Serve as the primary administrative support resource for the Center for College Readiness, providing day-to-day operational and customer service support across all programs. Process student applications and supporting documentation, ensuring records are complete, accurate, and entered into appropriate systems in a timely manner. Monitor and manage the Center for College Readiness shared email inbox, triaging inquiries, responding to routine requests, and routing complex issues to the appropriate staff member. Answer incoming telephone calls and provide professional, knowledgeable assistance to prospective students, families, school partners, and other stakeholders. Prepare and distribute routine program communications, including admissions decisions, confirmation letters, enrollment materials, and other standardized correspondence. Coordinate the collection, organization, and processing of enrollment documentation for program students, including course selections, housing preferences, weekend activity selections, health forms, and other required participant information. Maintain accurate student and program records while ensuring compliance with institutional policies regarding confidentiality and data management. Process departmental purchasing requests, supply orders, invoices, reimbursements, and other administrative transactions in accordance with institutional procedures. Track inventory of program materials and office supplies and coordinate replenishment as needed. Assist with scheduling, meeting coordination, document preparation, filing, and other general administrative support activities. Provide direct administrative support to Center for College Readiness leadership, including document preparation, project coordination, and other assigned tasks. During live residential programs and Center for College Readiness events, provide operational support to program directors, including organizing materials, preparing spaces, coordinating logistics, and assisting with participant needs. Perform occasional errands and other operational support activities to ensure the efficient delivery of programs and services. Assist with and attend special projects, events, orientations, and program operations as assigned. Collaborate effectively with admissions, student affairs, finance, marketing, and other campus departments to support a seamless participant experience. Perform other duties as assigned in support of the mission and goals of the Center for College Readiness. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Education: High School Diploma or GED Experience: 1 + years administrative support experience PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Associate or bachelor’s degree or equivalent experience in business administration, communications, education or related field preferred. Understanding of admissions workflows, enrollment processes, and general student services practices preferred. Experience in administrative support, office management, or higher education operations preferred. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Familiarity with CRM systems (Salesforce preferred) and standard office software (Microsoft Office Suite, Google Workspace, or equivalents). Strong organizational and time-management skills, with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet deadlines reliably. Effective written and verbal communication skills for internal staff, students, and families in structured interactions. Attention to detail and accuracy in data entry, recordkeeping, and reporting. Proficiency in scheduling, coordinating meetings, and managing calendars. Ability to follow established workflows and standard operating procedures independently. Comfort with technology and ability to learn new systems quickly. Ability to collaborate effectively with multiple internal teams (Admissions, Marketing, Enrollment Services) and escalate issues appropriately. Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive information with discretion. Ability to prioritize tasks, adapt to changing needs, and support departmental goals. Willingness to support occasional events or peak-period activities as directed. Work Environment This role operates in a professional office environment with regular interaction across campus and digital platforms. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Additional Information This description is intended to outline the general scope of responsibilities and is not an exhaustive list of duties. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a78859f9d4aea0c019d4c306cff1ae8&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "Admissions and Recruitment Mentor",
      "description": "Position: Admissions and Recruitment Mentor Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 165 # of Openings: 2 Job Title Admissions and Recruitment Mentor Location Remote - Maryland region, Remote - Chicago region Department Admissions Supervisor Title Director of Admissions Division Enrollment FLSA Classification Exempt Job Grade TBD Position Type Level Professional JOB SUMMARY: The Admissions & Recruitment Mentor is responsible for driving student enrollment through strategic outreach, relationship building, and application management within an assigned region. This role serves as a primary contact for prospective students and families, guiding them through the admissions process while representing Beacon College’s mission and programs. This position manages a regional recruitment territory and supports prospective students from initial inquiry through application completion, with a focus on increasing applications, enrollment, and overall pipeline growth. DUTIES AND TASKS: Develop and execute a regional recruitment strategy to generate prospective student interest and applications for college readiness and undergraduate programs. Conduct outreach to prospective students, families, schools, and professionals through phone, email, text, virtual meetings, and in-person events. Represent Beacon College at high schools, conferences, college fairs, and professional events. Build and maintain relationships with school counselors, educational consultants, and related professionals. Guide prospective students and families through the admissions process, including application completion and program selection. Review applications and support admissions decision processes in collaboration with leadership. Maintain and manage a pipeline of prospective students within the CRM system. Track and report on recruitment activity, application progress, and enrollment outcomes. Participate in on-campus and virtual recruitment events, including open houses and information sessions. Collaborate with Marketing and Admissions leadership to support recruitment campaigns and initiatives. Weekend and evening availability is expected throughout the year for some aspects of the position, including application review, travel, and admissions events. Recruits and/or evaluates applicants for admission by speaking with, reading applications, and summarizing evaluations. Completes these routine tasks with guidance and instruction from others. Travels and recruits in an assigned geography, assisting with planning and delivering public presentations, special events, and other recruitment activities. Perform additional responsibilities as assigned. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Knowledge: Bachelor’s degree required. 1–3 years of experience in admissions, recruitment, education, marketing, or a related field (required). Experience working with students with learning differences or ADHD (preferred). Skills: Strong public speaking and presentation skills. Excellent interpersonal and relationship-building abilities. Strong organizational and time management skills. Ability to manage a high-volume prospect pipeline. Proficiency in CRM systems and communication tools. Abilities: Ability to work independently in a remote, regional environment. Ability to travel regularly, including evenings and weekends. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with diverse stakeholders. Ability to maintain a professional and patient approach when working with students and families. ENVIRONMENT: The work environment characteristics described here are a representation of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodation(s) may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The term “qualified individual with a disability” means an individual with a disability who, with or without reasonable accommodation(s), can perform the essential functions of this position. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to communicate professionally in person, over the telephone, through email and other electronic means, move about the office and college, handle various types of media and equipment, and visually or otherwise identify, observe and assess. The employee is occasionally required to lift up to 15 pounds unless otherwise specified in the job description. NOTICE: The intent of this job description is to provide a representation of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions given this title and shall not be construed as a declaration of the total specific duties and responsibilities of any particular position. Employees may be directed to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically presented in this description. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and embraces diversity as a critical step in ensuring employee, student and graduate success. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a7883a8a0abfc6b01a0c9e2aec47f84&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "Summer Programs Residence Life Coordinator",
      "description": "Position: Summer Programs Residence Life Coordinator Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 213 # of Openings: 2 Make a difference in the lives of students at Beacon College. As the Summer Programs Residence Life Coordinator, you will help oversee the residential experience for summer program students, supporting housing operations, residential staff, student development, safety, and emergency response in a collaborative, student-centered environment. TITLE: Summer Programs Residence Life Coordinator DEPARTMENT: Enrollment Management WORK TYPE: Seasonal | Staff LOCATION: Beacon College, Leesburg, Florida FLSA: Exempt DIVISION: Enrollment Management JOB SUMMARY: The Summer Programs Residence Life Coordinator serves as a key member of the Beacon College summer programs team and collaboratively oversees the residential experience and housing operations for students participating in summer programs. This role is responsible for coordinating student and staff housing assignments, residential staffing, residence hall opening and closing processes, student support, safety and emergency response, and initiatives that promote student development and independent living skills. The Residence Life Coordinator works collaboratively with Resident Directors, Residence Life Assistants, academic staff, the Student Engagement Coordinator, Facilities partners, the Housing and Residence Life department, and other program administrators to create a safe, supportive, and well-organized residential environment. This position also helps ensure that residential facilities are prepared for program operations, appropriately maintained throughout the summer, and returned to appropriate condition for the academic year. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Collaborate with the other Residence Life Coordinator to oversee residential operations, including student and staff housing assignments. Recruit, hire, train, supervise, and support Resident Directors and Residence Life Assistants. Coordinate residential staffing schedules, including the emergency on-call phone and on-call rotation. Coordinate residence hall opening and closing processes, ensuring facilities are prepared for student arrival and appropriately reset following program completion. Work with Facilities partners and the Housing and Residence Life department to address building, maintenance, cleanliness, and operational needs and ensure residence halls are returned to appropriate condition for the academic year. Serve as an Administrator on Call during assigned daytime and evening hours and respond appropriately to residential and program emergencies. Serve as the lead interventionist for residence life concerns, including student needs, behavioral concerns, and conflicts. Collaborate with academic, student engagement, and program staff to address student concerns and coordinate appropriate support. Participate in at least one off-campus trip during each weekend of program operation. Attend and actively participate in required staff meetings and facilitate Residence Life meetings as needed. Present residence life-related information, expectations, and procedures during staff training. Create and maintain processes for collecting and evaluating information related to students’ residential experiences and development of independent living skills. Maintain a thorough understanding of and comply with all program policies, expectations, and procedures. Perform additional responsibilities and other duties as assigned in support of the mission and goals of Beacon College summer programs. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Education: High School Diploma or GED Experience: 2+ years of experience in residence life, student affairs, higher education, student services, or a related field. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Experience in residence life, student affairs, housing operations, student development, or a related higher education setting preferred. Experience supervising, training, or supporting residential or student staff preferred. Experience with student support, conflict resolution, crisis response, or emergency management preferred. Experience working with college students, transition programs, summer programs, or residential programming preferred. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Knowledge of residence life, student development, residential operations, and college transition practices. Knowledge of student support, independent living skills, residential supervision, emergency response, and risk management. Knowledge of housing assignments, residence hall operations, and facilities coordination. Strong organizational, supervisory, interpersonal, and problem-solving skills. Effective written and verbal communication skills when working with students, staff, campus departments, and external partners. Ability to supervise, train, and support residential staff effectively. Ability to coordinate housing assignments, staffing schedules, and residence hall opening and closing processes. Strong crisis response, conflict resolution, and decision-making skills. Ability to identify student needs and coordinate appropriate interventions and support. Ability to collect, organize, and evaluate student residential and independent living data. Ability to collaborate effectively with students, residential staff, academic staff, and campus partners. Ability to remain flexible, responsive, organized, and professional in a dynamic summer program environment. Ability to respond appropriately to emergencies and time-sensitive situations. Availability and willingness to work evenings, weekends, off-campus trips, and on-call assignments as required. WORK ENVIRONMENT: This role operates in a professional educational and residential campus environment with regular interaction with students, residential staff, program administrators, and other members of the Beacon College community. The position requires regular communication in person, by telephone, through email, and through other electronic means, as well as frequent movement throughout residence halls, offices, and the college campus. The employee may occasionally be required to lift up to 15 pounds. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. ADDITIONAL INFORMATION: This description is intended to outline the general scope of responsibilities and is not an exhaustive list of duties. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a78839ea1ccddec01a1cfd6440b13d6&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "Executive Director Human Resources",
      "description": "Position: Executive Director Human Resources Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 221 # of Openings: 1 Join Our Team At Beacon College, the nation's first accredited college dedicated exclusively to educating students with neurodivergent profiles, you'll have the opportunity to shape workplace culture and support the people who make our mission possible. We are seeking a collaborative Human Resources leader who thrives in a relationship-driven environment and is passionate about developing talent, fostering employee engagement, and helping our community create transformative outcomes for students. TITLE: Executive Director Human Resources DEPARTMENT: Human Resources WORK TYPE: Full-Time | Staff LOCATION: Beacon College, Leesburg, Florida FLSA: Exempt Salary DIVISION: Human Resources JOB SUMMARY: The Executive Director of Human Resources provides leadership and operational oversight for all Human Resources functions at Beacon College. Reporting to the President, this hands-on role leads talent management, employee relations, benefits, and compliance initiatives. The Executive Director fosters an inclusive, engaged workplace culture that supports the College's mission and goals. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND responsibilities: Leadership and Administration Align human resources strategies, programs, and initiatives with the College's mission, values, and institutional goals. Serve as a partner and advisor to campus leadership on workforce planning, organizational effectiveness, and employee-related matters. Lead employee engagement, recognition, and culture-building initiatives. Direct the operations, budget, priorities, and resource allocation of the Human Resources Department. Supervise, mentor, and evaluate Human Resource staff while fostering a high-performing, service-oriented team. Develop, implement, and continuously improve HR policies, procedures, and practices. Talent Management and Employee Development Oversee recruitment, hiring, onboarding, and retention efforts. Lead the College's performance management and employee development programs. Support professional development opportunities for faculty and staff across the institution. Compensation and Benefits Administer compensation programs and conduct market analysis to support competitive and equitable pay practices. Oversee employee benefits programs, including health, retirement, wellness, leave, and tuition benefits. Recommend strategies that support employee attraction, retention, and engagement while maintaining fiscal responsibility. Employee Relations and Compliance Provide leadership and guidance in resolving employee relations issues, workplace concerns, and grievances. Interpret and administer personnel policies. Ensure compliance with applicable federal and state employment laws and regulations, including EEO, ADA, FMLA, Title IX, wage and hour laws, and related requirements. Oversee accommodations, and other sensitive employee matters with professionalism and discretion. Promote a culture of respect, inclusion, accountability, and excellent customer service. HR Operations and Continuous Improvement Direct HR systems, records management, reporting, and personnel administration processes. Identify opportunities to improve efficiency, service delivery, and employee experience. Represent the College in professional organizations, community partnerships, and external HR-related activities. Perform other duties consistent with the scope and responsibilities of the position. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Education: Bachelor's degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, Public Administration, Higher Education Administration, or a related field. Experience: Four (4) years of progressively responsible human resources experience, including leadership and supervisory responsibilities. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Master's degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, Higher Education Administration, or a related field. PHR, SPHR, SHRM-CP, or SHRM-SCP certification. Human resources leadership experience in higher education or a similarly complex organization. Experience with HRIS, payroll, and enterprise systems in an educational environment. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Comprehensive knowledge of human resources management, employee relations, compensation and benefits, talent acquisition, organizational development, and federal and state employment laws. Strong leadership, problem-solving, decision-making, and conflict-resolution skills. Excellent verbal, written, presentation, negotiation, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to build trust, influence outcomes, and develop collaborative relationships with employees, leaders, and external partners. Demonstrated ability to handle sensitive and confidential matters with professionalism, discretion, and sound judgment. Strong service orientation and commitment to supporting a diverse and inclusive campus community. Proficiency with HRIS platforms, Microsoft 365 applications, and reporting tools. Knowledge of process improvement, system optimization, and continuous improvement practices. Commitment to creating an inclusive and supportive environment that advances the success of neurodiverse students, faculty, and staff. Ability to work occasional evenings, weekends, and emergency response situations as needed. ENVIRONMENT: This role operates in a professional office environment with regular interaction across campus and digital platforms. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Additional Information This description is intended to outline the general scope of responsibilities and is not an exhaustive list of duties. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    }
  },
